<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/069L">Example <span data-tag="069L">4.7.1.12</span> <span class="named">(Truncated Objects of Products)</span>.</a> Let $\{  \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_ i \} _{i \in I}$ be a collection of $\infty $-categories having product $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}= \prod _{i \in I} \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_ i$, and let $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an object given by a collection of objects $\{  X_ i \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_ i \} _{i \in I}$. Then $X$ is $n$-truncated if and only if each $X_ i$ is an $n$-truncated object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_ i$. See Remark <a data-tag="054U" href="/tag/054U">3.5.7.6</a>. </p>
